import type { ResearchPost } from './types';

export const november08ResearchPosts: readonly ResearchPost[] = [{"slug":"how-should-a-bdr-team-measure-reply-quality-beyond-reply-rate","title":"How should a BDR team measure reply quality beyond reply rate?","summary":"A reply-quality method that classifies responses, scores their substance, and connects them to real movement without overclaiming.","cluster":"Outreach operations","publishedAt":"2026-11-08","finding":"A reply rate is only a count of responses, while reply quality requires a documented category, a substance score, and a follow-up decision that can be reviewed independently of the sender.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-08/how-should-a-bdr-team-measure-reply-quality-beyond-reply-rate.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks how a BDR team should measure the quality of replies rather than only the share of messages that receive one. The unit is one reply to one outbound touch, classified on the day it is received. The measure combines the reply category, the substance of the response, and whether it moves the account to a defined next state. The scope excludes open rates, click rates, and other delivery signals, which describe attention rather than a buyer's decision.","The question matters because a reply rate can rise while the quality of conversations falls. A team can generate many one-word replies by asking a trivial question. It can also miss valuable silence if the only goal is a response. A quality measure protects the team from optimizing the easy count and losing the conversations that take longer to develop."]},{"heading":"Why reply rate hides variation","paragraphs":["A reply rate treats a polite no, a request for a proposal, and an angry objection as the same event. Those replies have very different implications for the queue and for the offer. A team that counts them together cannot tell whether its message is working. The count also ignores the BDR's response time and the accuracy of the next step, which shape the buyer's experience.","Another problem is that reply rate depends heavily on list quality and offer fit. A team with a narrow, well-researched list may get fewer replies than a team sending to a broad list, even though the first team produces better conversations. Comparing the two teams by reply rate alone is misleading. The measure needs to account for the population and the intent behind the outreach."]},{"heading":"Define reply categories","paragraphs":["Start with a small set of categories that a reviewer can apply consistently. Useful categories include interested, not now, not a fit, wrong person, request for information, and stop contact. Each category should have a definition and an example. The categories should be mutually exclusive enough that two reviewers usually agree on the same reply.","Separate a negative reply from a stop request. A buyer who says the timing is wrong may be a future opportunity, while a buyer who asks not to be contacted again requires immediate suppression. Mixing the two creates compliance risk and loses a legitimate follow-up. The category should determine the next action, not just describe the tone of the message."]},{"heading":"Score the substance of a reply","paragraphs":["Score whether the reply contains a specific problem, a question, a constraint, a timeline, or a referral to another person. A reply with one concrete element is more useful than a reply that only acknowledges the message. Use a simple scale such as none, general, and specific, and record the element that earned the score. The score should be based on what the buyer wrote, not on what the BDR hopes it means.","Keep the scoring separate from the category. A short interested reply can be specific, while a long polite reply can be general. The two dimensions answer different questions. The category says what the buyer decided, and the substance score says how much the team learned. Reporting them together gives a clearer picture than either one alone."]},{"heading":"Measure movement, not just sentiment","paragraphs":["A positive reply that never leads to a next step has not moved the account. Record whether the reply produced a scheduled call, a requested document, a referral, or a documented close. Movement is a stronger signal than sentiment because it requires the buyer to take an action. The measure should use the same definitions as the pipeline stages so the result is comparable across teams.","Do not count a BDR's internal note as movement unless it records a buyer commitment. A note that says the buyer seemed interested is a judgment, not a next state. A note that says the buyer agreed to a call on a specific date is movement. The distinction keeps the measure honest and makes it useful for forecasting."]},{"heading":"Segment by source and sequence","paragraphs":["Break the results down by lead source, sequence, account tier, and BDR. A pattern that appears only in one segment is easier to diagnose than a queue-wide average. A high reply rate from one source with low substance may mean the source attracts curiosity rather than need. A low reply rate from a high-value segment may mean the message is not relevant enough.","Compare sequences at the same stage and with the same offer. A sequence that includes a strong proof point may produce better replies than one that only asks for time. The comparison should hold the list quality constant where possible. Without that control, the team may attribute a result to the message when the list was the real difference."]},{"heading":"Sample design and bias","paragraphs":["Review a sample of replies that includes positive, negative, and no-response threads. A sample of only positive replies will overstate quality and hide the reasons for rejection. Include replies from different BDRs and different weeks. Record the sample size and the selection rule before scoring so the result cannot be shaped after the fact.","Check whether the sample over-represents easy-to-classify replies. A one-word yes is easy to score, while a long ambiguous message may be skipped. The skipped messages are often the most informative. Review them separately and record why they were hard to classify. That note is a useful input for improving the category definitions."]},{"heading":"Reading results without overclaiming","paragraphs":["Report the category mix, the substance distribution, and the share of replies that produced movement. Compare the result with the previous period and with a comparable segment. Do not claim that a message caused a change unless the team tested the change deliberately. A correlation between a new subject line and a better reply mix may have another cause.","Use confidence language that matches the sample. A small sample can suggest a question for the next test, but it cannot prove a general rule. If the team needs a stronger conclusion, run a controlled test with a clear hypothesis and a stable list. The report should state what the data can and cannot support."]},{"heading":"Reporting and decisions","paragraphs":["Put the reply-quality view in the same review as pipeline movement so the team sees both the input and the outcome. A page with three panels is usually enough: category mix, substance and movement, and a short list of examples. The examples make the numbers concrete and give the team something to discuss. Keep the report short enough to use in a working session.","Choose one action from the review, such as improving a question, changing a list source, or coaching a reply category. Measure the same indicators after the change. If the change does not help, record that result rather than dropping the measure. The goal is a learning loop, not a dashboard that grows every month."]},{"heading":"Coach from reply examples","paragraphs":["Use anonymized reply examples in the monthly review so the team can see what a specific reply looks like. Pick one strong example and one ambiguous example, then ask the team to classify and score them. The discussion exposes differences in interpretation before they affect the data. A shared example is more useful than another definition document.","Coach the response, not only the classification. A BDR who receives a vague reply can ask one clarifying question instead of guessing the buyer's intent. Review whether the follow-up question was appropriate and timely. The quality of the reply often depends on the quality of the next question, not only on the first message."]},{"heading":"Avoid metric drift","paragraphs":["Revisit the category definitions and the substance scale every quarter. A definition that was clear when it was written may become ambiguous as the team works with new reply types. Record any change and apply it to a new sample rather than rescoring old replies under the new rule. Mixing standards makes the trend unreadable.","Do not change the measure every time the result is inconvenient. A stable measure with a known limitation is more useful than a perfect measure that keeps moving. If the team needs a new view, add it alongside the existing one for a period and compare. The goal is learning, not a dashboard that always looks favorable."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The method depends on consistent classification, which takes calibration and time. A busy team may score replies quickly and introduce drift. The substance score is a judgment and may reflect the reviewer's expectations. The sample may miss replies that arrived through a channel the team does not track. State these limits when the results are shared.","The measure also cannot capture the value of a conversation that takes months to develop. A reply that looks low-substance today may become important after a change in the buyer's business. Keep the qualitative notes and revisit them when the account returns. A useful measure should support judgment, not replace it."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission, CAN-SPAM Act compliance guide","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"}]},{"slug":"what-belongs-in-an-account-research-audit-trail","title":"What belongs in an account research audit trail?","summary":"A field-level standard for account research records, with provenance, versioning, access rules, and a review test.","cluster":"Market intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-11-08","finding":"An account research audit trail is defensible when every material field has a source, a check date, a version, an owner, and an access rule that a reviewer can apply without asking the researcher.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-08/what-belongs-in-an-account-research-audit-trail.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what an account research record needs so that a reviewer can reconstruct how a conclusion was reached. The unit is one account research record used to support outreach, qualification, or a handoff. The trail covers the fields that affect a decision, such as company size, buyer role, trigger, fit rationale, and contact source. It excludes casual notes and speculative ideas that were never used.","The question matters because research records often mix fact, inference, and memory in a single paragraph. A reader cannot tell which part came from a public source and which part was the researcher's guess. That ambiguity makes the record hard to defend when a buyer or a manager challenges it. A clear trail turns a private impression into a reviewable piece of work."]},{"heading":"Why a trail differs from notes","paragraphs":["Notes are written for the person who did the research, while a trail is written for someone who did not. Notes can use shorthand, private references, and assumptions that the author understands. A trail needs enough context for a cold reader to follow the same path. The difference is not length, because a short trail can be complete and a long note can be vague.","A trail also records what changed. A note may be edited silently, while a trail keeps the previous value and the reason for the update. That history matters when a decision was based on an earlier version. Without it, the team cannot tell whether the account was qualified on current evidence or on a fact that has since been corrected."]},{"heading":"Minimum fields for a material claim","paragraphs":["Every material claim should have a field for the value, the source, the source date, and the date the researcher checked it. Add the researcher's name and a short note on how the source supports the value. If the value is an inference, label it as an inference and name the facts it rests on. These fields are enough for most account research.","Separate the source from the evidence. A company website is a source, while a specific page or filing is the evidence. Link to the evidence rather than only naming the source. If the evidence is a conversation, record who said it and when. A reviewer should be able to open the link or identify the person without searching the inbox."]},{"heading":"Versioning and change history","paragraphs":["Keep a version number or a timestamp for each material field. When a value changes, record the old value, the new value, the reason, and the date. Do not overwrite the history unless the change is a simple typo. The history should show whether the account was re-researched or corrected after a review.","If a source disappears, record that it was unavailable rather than deleting the claim. A broken link is a finding about source durability, not a reason to erase the record. Add a replacement source when one is found. The trail should show the evolution of the evidence, including its gaps."]},{"heading":"Access and privacy","paragraphs":["Define who can see each field and why. Contact details, personal notes, and commercial terms may need stricter access than firmographic data. Use the least access necessary for the work. Record the access rule in the record or in a linked policy so a reviewer knows what can be shared.","Do not put sensitive personal information into a research note that may be copied into a shared document. Follow the team's privacy policy and any contractual requirement. If a field is needed for a decision, link to the controlled source instead of copying it. The trail should support the decision without creating a second uncontrolled copy."]},{"heading":"The cold-read test","paragraphs":["Give the record to a reviewer who did not do the research and ask them to explain the account's fit and the evidence behind it. The reviewer should be able to identify the source of each material claim and the open questions. If they cannot, the trail is incomplete. The test should use a real decision, not a hypothetical one.","Record the reviewer's questions and use them to improve the template. If the same question appears across records, the template is missing a field. If the question is specific to one account, a note may be enough. The test is cheap and repeatable, and it turns a vague standard into a concrete one."]},{"heading":"Review cadence","paragraphs":["Review a sample of records each month and check the fields that matter most. Look for claims without sources, sources without dates, and inferences presented as facts. A small sample of ten records is enough to reveal a pattern. Record the findings by field and by researcher so the team can see where the gaps concentrate.","Use the review to update the template and the training. If a field is consistently incomplete, ask whether it is useful or whether the prompt is unclear. Remove fields that no one uses. A simpler trail that people maintain is better than a detailed one that gets skipped."]},{"heading":"Retention and deletion","paragraphs":["Decide how long each type of research record should be kept. A short-lived market signal may need a shorter retention period than a contract-related record. Follow the team's legal and contractual requirements. Record the retention rule so a reviewer does not have to guess.","Delete records according to the rule rather than keeping everything forever. A large archive of stale research creates risk and makes the current record harder to find. Before deleting, check whether the record supports a decision that is still active. If it does, keep the relevant evidence and remove the rest."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the share of records that pass the cold-read test, the fields most often missing, and the sources that are most fragile. Add one anonymized example of a strong trail and one weak one. The examples make the standard concrete and give the team something to copy. Keep the report short enough to discuss in a working session.","Use the findings to change one part of the template or the review. Then test a new sample and compare. The goal is a record that a reviewer can trust without a conversation. A follow-up test shows whether the change worked, and it keeps the trail from becoming a one-time documentation project."]},{"heading":"Working with third-party data","paragraphs":["When a paid database supplies a field, record the database name, the query, and the date the result was pulled. The database may update or change its coverage, so the same query can produce a different answer later. A reviewer should be able to reproduce the pull or at least see what was asked. Do not present a database value as if it came from the company itself.","Check important third-party values against a second source when the decision depends on them. If the sources disagree, record both values and the conflict. The conflict is useful evidence because it shows where the record is uncertain. A researcher who hides the disagreement makes the record look cleaner and less trustworthy."]},{"heading":"When a source is challenged","paragraphs":["If a buyer or a manager challenges a source, do not defend it reflexively. Reopen the evidence, check the date, and ask what the challenger knows that the record does not. Record the challenge and the resolution. A corrected record is more valuable than a record that appears consistent.","If the source cannot be verified, downgrade the claim rather than deleting it. Label it as unverified and keep the link so the next reviewer can see the gap. A visible gap is easier to manage than a hidden assumption. The team should be able to distinguish a fact that was checked from a fact that was merely repeated."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A trail cannot prove that a claim is true, only that the team recorded where it came from. The source may be wrong, outdated, or biased. The reviewer may interpret the evidence differently from the researcher. The sample may miss records that were never used in a decision. State these limits when the results are shared.","The standard also adds work, and a team under pressure may treat it as a formality. Keep the fields proportional to the decision and review the burden regularly. If the trail is too heavy, the team will fill it with placeholders. A light but enforced standard is more useful than a perfect one that is ignored."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]},{"slug":"when-should-a-bdr-team-retire-a-market-segment","title":"When should a BDR team retire a market segment?","summary":"A retirement test for market segments, with demand signals, cost-to-serve measures, and a transition plan that preserves learning.","cluster":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-08","finding":"A market segment should be retired when demand, reply quality, and cost to serve stay below the team's threshold across several periods even after the message, list, and routing have been repaired.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-08/when-should-a-bdr-team-retire-a-market-segment.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks when a BDR team should stop working a market segment rather than continue to invest in it. The unit is one segment defined by industry, company size, region, or another stable characteristic. The evidence includes demand, reply quality, cost to serve, and the team's ability to reach the right buyer. The scope excludes individual accounts that fail for account-specific reasons.","The question matters because segments are easy to add and hard to retire. A segment may have been promising when it was added, but the market, the offer, or the team's access may have changed. Continuing to work it consumes capacity that could go to a better segment. A clear retirement test protects the team from sunk-cost thinking."]},{"heading":"Define what retirement means","paragraphs":["Retirement does not necessarily mean never contacting the segment again. It can mean pausing outbound work, moving the segment to a nurture list, or closing it until a specific trigger appears. Each option has a different cost and a different review date. The team should choose the option deliberately rather than letting the segment fade into an unowned list.","Write the retirement decision in a short record that names the segment, the evidence, the date, and the condition for reopening. That record prevents the same debate from returning every quarter. It also gives a new owner the context they need if the segment is reconsidered. A clear decision is more useful than a vague feeling that the segment did not work."]},{"heading":"Check demand before blaming the message","paragraphs":["Start with the demand signal. Is the segment large enough to support the team's target, and is there evidence that the problem exists? A segment can be small but valuable, so size alone is not the test. Look for a pattern in the accounts that did engage, not only in the ones that did not.","If the demand signal is weak, a better message will not create a market. If the demand signal is strong but replies are weak, the message or the list may be the problem. The distinction determines whether the team should repair the approach or retire the segment. Do not retire a strong market because of a weak first sequence."]},{"heading":"Measure reply quality and movement","paragraphs":["Use the reply-quality categories from the team's outreach review, not only the reply rate. Look for specific replies, requests for information, and movement to a next state. A segment that produces many polite acknowledgments but no movement is not working, even if the reply rate looks healthy. A segment with fewer replies but better conversations may deserve more time.","Segment the results by account tier and buyer role. A segment may work for one role and fail for another. If the team is reaching the wrong person, the problem may be the contact source rather than the market. Test a different role before retiring the whole segment. The result should identify which part of the segment is failing."]},{"heading":"Calculate the cost to serve","paragraphs":["Estimate the research time, message preparation, reply handling, and review time per qualified conversation. Compare that cost with the value of the opportunities the segment produces. A segment that requires much more research for the same result may be a poor use of capacity even if it is technically a fit. The comparison should use a consistent time period.","Include the hidden cost of context switching. A segment that requires a different vocabulary, compliance rule, or tool may add coordination work beyond the direct research time. If the team cannot specialize in the segment, the cost may be higher than the log shows. A simple cost estimate is enough to support a decision, as long as the assumptions are visible."]},{"heading":"Check source and access decay","paragraphs":["A segment may fail because the team can no longer reach the right contacts. A data source may have become stale, a directory may have closed, or a platform may have changed its rules. Check whether the contact data is still accurate and whether the team can verify the buyer's role. If access is the constraint, retiring the segment may be premature.","Review the source mix for the segment and the share of records that pass a basic accuracy check. A high bounce rate or a high share of wrong-person replies points to a data problem. A low reply rate with accurate contacts points to a relevance problem. The two need different repairs."]},{"heading":"Test before retiring","paragraphs":["Before retiring a segment, run a small controlled test with a repaired message, a different role, or a new source. The test should have a clear hypothesis and a fixed sample. Do not change several variables at once if the team needs to know which one mattered. A short test can save a segment that was written off too early.","If the test fails, record the result and retire the segment with evidence. If it succeeds, keep the change and set a review date. A test is not a delay if it answers a real question. It is a way to avoid retiring a good market because of an avoidable execution problem."]},{"heading":"Plan the transition","paragraphs":["When a segment is retired, decide what happens to the existing accounts and contacts. Some accounts may move to a nurture list with a trigger. Some may be reassigned to a different segment. Some may need a final note to the buyer. The transition should be planned so the team does not leave open commitments or unanswered replies.","Tell the team why the segment is being retired and what work replaces it. A retirement without a replacement can feel like a loss of capacity rather than a reallocation. Show the hours or attention that will move to a stronger segment. That framing helps the team see the decision as a choice about focus, not a judgment about their effort."]},{"heading":"Reporting and review","paragraphs":["Report the segment's demand, reply quality, movement, cost to serve, and source accuracy for the periods reviewed. Add the test result and the chosen option. Keep the report short enough to use in a planning meeting. A table by segment is more useful than a single average across all segments.","Set a review date for the retirement decision, even if the segment is closed. A market can change, and a new trigger may justify a fresh look. The review should ask whether the original reason still holds. A decision that is never revisited can become as stale as the segment itself."]},{"heading":"Reallocate the capacity","paragraphs":["When a segment is retired, show where its research time, message volume, and review attention will go. A retirement that does not free capacity for a stronger segment is just a reduction. Name the receiving segment or queue and the expected change. The team should see the tradeoff as a choice about focus.","Protect the receiving queue from becoming a dumping ground for every displaced account. A segment transfer can overload the next team if the accounts are not qualified. Review the transferred list and remove accounts that do not meet the receiving segment's criteria. A clean transfer is more useful than a large one."]},{"heading":"Communicate the decision","paragraphs":["Tell the team what was decided, what evidence was used, and when the decision will be reviewed. Avoid framing the retirement as a failure of the people who worked the segment. The market or the access may have changed, and the team's effort may still have produced useful learning. A factual explanation keeps morale intact.","Tell any partner or referrer who sends accounts from the retired segment so they are not surprised by a slower response. A short note about the change and the alternative path is enough. The relationship should not depend on the team continuing work that no longer fits. Clear communication prevents a quiet loss of trust."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["Segment results are affected by the list, the message, the timing, and the team's capacity. A retirement decision based on one quarter may reflect a temporary condition rather than a structural problem. The cost estimate may miss hidden work. The sample of qualified conversations may be small. State these limits when the decision is shared.","A segment can also be strategically important even when it is not efficient today. A new market, a partnership, or a regulatory change may justify continued investment. The retirement test should inform the decision, not make it automatically. The team should be able to explain why a strategically important segment is exempt."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"}]},{"slug":"how-much-pipeline-should-a-bdr-assistant-be-accountable-for","title":"How much pipeline should a BDR assistant be accountable for?","summary":"A role-design analysis that separates owned pipeline, influenced pipeline, and support work so an assistant is measured fairly.","cluster":"Role design","publishedAt":"2026-11-08","finding":"An assistant should be accountable for the quality and completeness of the work they own, while pipeline credit should follow the decision rights and the handoff evidence rather than a single revenue number.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-08/how-much-pipeline-should-a-bdr-assistant-be-accountable-for.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks how much pipeline a BDR assistant should be accountable for when the assistant prepares research, lists, messages, or CRM records. The unit is one defined work queue over a period, not an individual deal. The measure should separate work the assistant owns from work the assistant supports. The scope excludes licensed decisions and final commercial commitments, which stay with the accountable owner.","The question matters because a pipeline target can push an assistant into decisions the role was not designed to make. It can also hide the value of good preparation when the deal closes much later. A fair measure connects the assistant's work to the evidence and the handoff, not only to the revenue outcome. That connection protects quality and makes the role easier to manage."]},{"heading":"Why attribution is difficult","paragraphs":["Pipeline attribution is difficult because many people touch an account before a deal closes. The BDR, the assistant, the account executive, and the buyer all contribute at different stages. A simple source field may credit the first touch even when the decisive work happened later. A simple owner field may credit the closer even when the preparation made the conversation possible.","The difficulty is not a reason to avoid measurement. It is a reason to define what the team means by owned, influenced, and supported. Each category should have a rule that a reviewer can apply. Without those rules, the assistant's contribution becomes a matter of opinion, and the target becomes arbitrary."]},{"heading":"Define owned, influenced, and supported","paragraphs":["Owned work is the part of the queue the assistant is responsible for completing to a standard, such as a research record or a prepared list. Influenced pipeline is an opportunity where the assistant's work is documented as a material input, such as a source brief or a meeting note. Supported work is general help that does not have a direct line to a specific opportunity. The three categories need different measures.","Use the same definitions across the team so the assistant is not compared with a BDR on a different basis. If a BDR owns a set of accounts and the assistant prepares research for those accounts, the assistant does not own the pipeline. The assistant owns the research quality and the timeliness of the preparation. The distinction keeps the accountability proportional to the decision rights."]},{"heading":"Set the role boundary","paragraphs":["Write the boundary before setting a target. Name what the assistant can send, what needs review, what must be escalated, and what the assistant must never decide. A target that conflicts with the boundary will either be missed or push the assistant past the limit. The boundary should be visible in the queue and in the review.","Review the boundary when the workflow changes. A new offer, a new market, or a new tool can shift what is safe to delegate. A boundary that was right for a simple research queue may be wrong for a queue that includes pricing questions. Update the boundary and the measures together so the role stays coherent."]},{"heading":"Measure inputs and quality","paragraphs":["Measure the inputs the assistant controls, such as records completed, sources verified, lists prepared, and messages reviewed. Then measure quality with a sample, such as the share of records that pass a cold-read test or the share of messages that meet the standard. Inputs alone reward volume, while quality alone can hide capacity. The two views belong together.","Use a small set of measures that the assistant can influence directly. A measure that depends on the buyer's response may be affected by the list, the offer, or the timing. That does not make it useless, but it should not be the only basis for accountability. The assistant should be able to explain how their work contributed to the result."]},{"heading":"Use sample-based accountability","paragraphs":["Review a sample of the assistant's work each period and compare it with the standard. The sample should include completed, returned, and escalated items. A high completion count with a low pass rate is not good performance. A lower count with a high pass rate may be a better use of capacity if the queue is small.","Record the sample size and the criteria so the review is repeatable. If the assistant disagrees with a score, discuss the evidence rather than the number. The goal is a shared standard, not a ranking. A sample-based review also gives the assistant specific examples for improvement."]},{"heading":"Set targets that match the role","paragraphs":["Set targets for the work the assistant owns, such as the number of research records completed to standard or the share of handoffs that pass review. Add a secondary target for the quality of influenced opportunities only when the evidence is strong enough to attribute the contribution. A single pipeline number is usually too blunt for a support role. Put the target in writing with the review method so the assistant knows how the result will be judged.","Make the target achievable with the available sources and review capacity. A target that requires the assistant to skip verification will produce volume and rework. If the team needs more output, test a process change or add capacity before raising the target. The target should follow the capacity plan, not replace it."]},{"heading":"Review the attribution with the account owner","paragraphs":["Ask the account owner to confirm which assistant contributions were material to the opportunity. Use a short list of contribution types, such as research, list building, meeting preparation, or CRM cleanup. Record the confirmation in the opportunity. This gives the team a documented basis for influenced pipeline.","Do not ask the account owner to guess a percentage. A percentage is hard to defend and invites disagreement. A contribution type and a short note are enough for most reviews. If the team needs a financial view, use a rule that is agreed in advance and applied consistently. The rule should be simple enough to explain to the assistant."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report owned work quality, influenced pipeline by contribution type, and the share of work that was returned or escalated. Add a short note on the capacity and the source mix. These views show whether the assistant is doing the work the role was designed for. They also show where the boundary may need adjustment.","Use the findings to improve the workflow rather than to rank the assistant against a BDR. If the pass rate is low, check the template, the sources, and the review time. If the influenced pipeline is low, check whether the assistant has access to the accounts that matter. The goal is a role that contributes reliably, not a target that looks impressive."]},{"heading":"Onboard the assistant to the measure","paragraphs":["Explain the difference between owned, influenced, and supported work in the first week. Use real examples from the queue so the assistant can see how each category is recorded. Ask the assistant to classify a few completed items and discuss the results. A measure that is not understood cannot guide the work.","Give the assistant access to the evidence they need to do the job, such as the source log, the account record, and the review standard. A target without access creates frustration and shortcuts. If a tool or permission is missing, fix it before judging the output. The role should be set up to succeed before it is measured."]},{"heading":"Review the target after two quarters","paragraphs":["After two quarters, check whether the target still matches the queue and the role. A target that was right during a launch may be wrong after the launch ends. A target that was easy may have become hard because the sources changed. Update the target with evidence rather than leaving it fixed out of habit.","Ask the assistant what makes the work harder or easier than the target assumes. The answer may reveal a tool problem, a source problem, or a review bottleneck. Adjust the conditions before changing the number. A target should describe a realistic standard, not a wish that ignores the operating environment."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["Attribution will always be imperfect because pipeline is a team outcome. A contribution note is a judgment, and different owners may apply it differently. The sample may miss work that helped indirectly. The target may be affected by market conditions outside the assistant's control. State these limits when the results are shared.","A narrow focus on pipeline can also discourage the maintenance work that keeps the queue healthy. Research records, source logs, and clean handoffs may not produce immediate pipeline, but they prevent rework later. The measurement should include those foundations or the role will drift toward visible activity. Review the foundations with the same sample discipline used for the direct output."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"}]},{"slug":"what-makes-a-meeting-handoff-audit-repeatable","title":"What makes a meeting handoff audit repeatable?","summary":"A repeatable audit design for meeting handoffs, with stage definitions, evidence fields, calibration, and a repair loop.","cluster":"Account strategy","publishedAt":"2026-11-08","finding":"A meeting handoff audit is repeatable when it uses fixed stage definitions, a stable sample, a small evidence rubric, and a calibration step that lets two reviewers reach the same conclusion.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-08/what-makes-a-meeting-handoff-audit-repeatable.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what makes an audit of meeting handoffs repeatable across reviewers and periods. The unit is one handoff from a BDR to an account executive or from one owner to another. The audit covers the context transferred, the commitments recorded, and the next step accepted by the receiving owner. The scope excludes the quality of the meeting itself, which is a separate review.","The question matters because handoffs are often judged by whether the receiving owner complains. That signal is useful but inconsistent. A quiet owner may have enough context, or may simply not have read the record. A repeatable audit gives the team a shared standard and a way to see whether a change improved the handoff."]},{"heading":"Define the handoff stages","paragraphs":["Break the handoff into stages such as prepared, sent, received, accepted, and acted on. Each stage should have a timestamp and an owner. A handoff that was sent but never accepted is not complete. A handoff that was accepted but never acted on may reveal a capacity problem rather than a context problem.","Use the same stage names in the CRM and in the audit. If the CRM uses different terms, map them in the audit guide so reviewers do not invent a new vocabulary. A shared vocabulary makes the sample easier to draw and the results easier to compare. It also helps the team see where the handoff stalls."]},{"heading":"Choose the evidence fields","paragraphs":["Select a small set of fields that a receiver needs to act, such as the buyer's problem, the people involved, the commitments made, the next step, and the source of any material claim. Each field should have a definition and an example. A receiver should be able to act from the record without a second conversation. If a field does not change the action, leave it out of the audit sample.","Do not audit every field in the CRM. A long checklist makes the audit slow and encourages box-ticking. Choose the fields that change whether the receiver can act. If a field is consistently unused, remove it from the audit and consider removing it from the template. The audit should reflect the real work, not an ideal form."]},{"heading":"Draw a stable sample","paragraphs":["Sample handoffs across BDRs, account tiers, meeting types, and outcomes. Include handoffs that were accepted quickly and ones that stalled. A sample of only successful handoffs will overstate quality. Record the sample size, the date range, and the selection rule before reviewing.","Keep the sample size small enough to review carefully, such as ten to fifteen handoffs. A small sample reviewed consistently is more useful than a large sample reviewed once. If the team changes its meeting types or its CRM during the audit, start a new sample. A stable sample makes the comparison meaningful."]},{"heading":"Build a small rubric","paragraphs":["Use three levels for each evidence field: present and usable, present but incomplete, or missing. Add a separate overall judgment for whether the receiver could act without a follow-up conversation. The rubric should be simple enough to apply in a few minutes per handoff. It should also be specific enough that two reviewers usually agree.","Score the record, not the meeting. A good meeting can produce a weak handoff, and a difficult meeting can produce a strong one. The audit is about whether the context survived the handoff. Keeping that focus prevents the review from becoming a judgment of the BDR's performance in the room."]},{"heading":"Calibrate the reviewers","paragraphs":["Have two reviewers score the same small sample before the full audit. Compare the scores and discuss the differences. If the disagreement comes from a vague definition, rewrite it. If it comes from a different reading of the record, add an example to the guide. The goal is a shared standard, not perfect agreement.","Repeat the calibration when the team, the offer, or the meeting format changes. A rubric that worked for discovery calls may not fit a technical review. The calibration step is part of the audit, not an optional extra. A short session saves time later by preventing a debate about every score."]},{"heading":"Identify common failure modes","paragraphs":["Look for handoffs with no named receiver, no next step, or a commitment that is not recorded. These are the failures that most often cause a dropped follow-up. Also look for handoffs that contain a lot of text but no decision. Length is not a proxy for usefulness.","Check whether the receiver had to ask a question that the record should have answered. Those questions are the most direct evidence of a gap. Group them by field so the team can see which part of the template needs work. A recurring question is a stronger signal than a single low score."]},{"heading":"Run the repair loop","paragraphs":["Choose one failure mode from the audit and change one part of the template or the training. For example, add a required commitment field or a short example of a usable next step. Then audit a new sample and compare the same fields. Do not change several parts at once if the team needs to know what worked.","Record the change and the result in the audit guide so the next reviewer can see the history. If the change did not help, say so and try a different repair. A failed change is still useful evidence. The goal is a handoff that improves over time, not a perfect first version."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the sample size, the field-level results, the share of handoffs the receiver could act on, and the questions receivers asked. Add one anonymized strong example and one weak one. The examples make the standard concrete and give the team something to copy. Keep the report short enough to use in a working session.","Use the findings to improve the handoff and the review rhythm. If the same gap appears every month, the template or the training is not working. If the gap changes, the team may be improving or the sample may be different. Compare like with like before drawing a conclusion."]},{"heading":"Tooling for the audit","paragraphs":["Keep the audit in a simple form that the reviewers can complete without a new system. A spreadsheet with one row per handoff and a column per field is enough for a small team. Link each row to the source record so a reviewer can check the evidence. The tool should reduce effort, not add a second workflow. Add a short notes column for anything the rubric does not capture.","If the team already has a quality review tool, use it rather than creating a parallel process. The audit should fit the existing rhythm and vocabulary. A separate tool that nobody opens will not produce a repeatable result. The best tool is the one the reviewers already use."]},{"heading":"Using the audit in onboarding","paragraphs":["Use a few anonymized handoffs as training examples for new BDRs. Ask the new BDR to identify what the receiver needed and what was missing. The exercise teaches the standard before the first real handoff. It also gives the reviewer a baseline for the new BDR's early work.","Update the examples when the template changes so the training does not teach an old standard. Keep the examples short and paired with the actual record. A new BDR should be able to see the difference between a usable handoff and a weak one. The audit becomes a teaching tool rather than a surprise inspection. Ask the new BDR to rewrite the weak example and compare it with the original."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["The audit measures the record, not the relationship or the buyer's experience. A receiver may act successfully despite a weak record because they already know the account. The sample may miss handoffs that were never logged. The reviewers may share a blind spot. State these limits when the results are shared.","A repeatable audit also takes time, and a busy team may rush it. Keep the sample small and the rubric short so the audit can survive a busy month. A simple audit done consistently is more valuable than a detailed one that happens once. The standard should be demanding enough to matter and light enough to repeat."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Marketing and sales","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/marketing-sales"},{"label":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Sales Managers","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/sales-managers.htm"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"}]},{"slug":"how-should-a-bdr-team-document-competitive-displacement-risk","title":"How should a BDR team document competitive displacement risk?","summary":"A documentation standard for competitive displacement risk, with evidence levels, account signals, and a review cadence.","cluster":"Market intelligence","publishedAt":"2026-11-08","finding":"Competitive displacement risk is documented defensibly when each signal has a source, a date, an account link, and a risk level that a reviewer can challenge without guessing what the BDR meant.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-08/how-should-a-bdr-team-document-competitive-displacement-risk.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks how a BDR team should record the risk that an existing vendor or an alternative approach will displace the team's offer in an account. The unit is one account and one competitive signal. The record should cover the signal, its source, the affected buyer, and the likely effect on the opportunity. The scope excludes general market commentary that is not tied to a specific account decision.","The question matters because displacement risk is often discussed in meetings and never written down. The team may know that a buyer is evaluating a competitor, but the knowledge stays with one person. When the account changes hands, the risk disappears from the record. A short documentation standard keeps the risk visible and reviewable."]},{"heading":"Define displacement risk","paragraphs":["Displacement risk is the chance that a buyer will choose or keep an alternative that reduces the value of the team's offer. The alternative can be a named competitor, an internal build, or a decision to do nothing. Each type has a different signal and a different response. The record should name the type rather than using a generic competitor label.","Separate displacement risk from ordinary competition. A buyer who is comparing options is not necessarily at risk of displacement. The risk rises when the buyer has an active alternative, a reason to prefer it, and a path to adopt it. The record should show which of those conditions is present, not just that a competitor was mentioned."]},{"heading":"Set evidence levels","paragraphs":["Use a small set of evidence levels such as confirmed, reported, and inferred. Confirmed means the buyer or a primary document states the alternative is in use or under active evaluation. Reported means a colleague or a public source says so, but the buyer has not confirmed it. Inferred means the team is reading a signal without direct evidence.","Attach a source and a date to every level. A confirmed signal from six months ago may no longer be true. A reported signal from a partner may be biased. The level should reflect the strength of the evidence, not the confidence of the person who wrote it. A reviewer should be able to see why the level was chosen."]},{"heading":"Identify account signals","paragraphs":["Common signals include a public vendor announcement, a job posting for a role tied to another tool, a buyer's comment about an existing contract, or a procurement document. A signal can also be a pattern, such as repeated questions about migration or a request for a comparison. The team should record the signal and the context, not just the conclusion. Write the signal in the buyer's words when a direct quote is available.","Not every signal means displacement. A buyer may mention a competitor to test the team's response, or may be locked into a contract for another year. The record should note the buyer's current state and the next review date. A signal without that context is easy to overread."]},{"heading":"Link the signal to the opportunity","paragraphs":["Attach the risk record to the account and the open opportunity so it appears in the pipeline review. Name the buyer contact who raised the signal and the date. If the signal affects the forecast, note the amount and the stage. A risk that lives only in a research document will not change a decision.","Separate the account-level risk from the opportunity-level risk. An account may be at risk of displacement in one division but not another. An opportunity may be exposed even if the account is not. The link should be specific enough that a reviewer can see which deal is affected."]},{"heading":"Use a short risk template","paragraphs":["A useful template has six fields: alternative, evidence level, source, buyer contact, effect on the opportunity, and next check date. Add a short note on the response, such as a proof point to send or a question to ask. The template should fit on one screen so the BDR will actually use it. Keep the latest version linked from the account so there is only one place to update.","Do not require a long narrative. A short, specific record is easier to review and update. If the situation is complex, link to the longer account notes. The template should capture the decision-relevant facts, not the entire history of the competitive landscape."]},{"heading":"Review the risk on a cadence","paragraphs":["Review open displacement risks in the same rhythm as the pipeline. A risk that has not changed in a quarter may be stale, while a risk that changed last week may need action. Record the review date and the reviewer. A risk that is never reviewed becomes a historical note rather than a live signal.","Use the review to update the evidence level and the next check date. If the buyer has confirmed the alternative, raise the level and adjust the forecast. If the signal has faded, lower the level or close the record. The review should be quick and focused on the accounts that matter."]},{"heading":"Escalate the highest-risk accounts","paragraphs":["Define what triggers an escalation, such as a confirmed alternative in a late-stage opportunity or a buyer request for a migration plan. Escalation should go to the owner with the authority to change the approach, not to a general channel. Record the escalation and the response so the team can see what happened. Set a response time so the risk does not wait in a queue.","Do not treat every competitor mention as an escalation. A flood of low-level signals makes the important one hard to see. Use the evidence level and the opportunity stage to decide. A confirmed risk in a large, late-stage deal deserves more attention than an inferred mention in an early conversation."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the number of open risks by level, the accounts with confirmed alternatives, and the share of risks reviewed on schedule. Add one anonymized example of a well-documented risk and one weak one. The examples make the standard concrete. Keep the report short enough to use in a pipeline review.","Use the findings to improve the template and the review. If many records lack a source, the template may be too easy to skip. If many risks are stale, the review cadence may be wrong. Change one part and measure the next cycle. The goal is a record that helps the team respond, not a competitive archive."]},{"heading":"Working with account owners","paragraphs":["Share the risk record with the account owner before the next buyer conversation. The owner may have context that changes the level or the response. Ask whether the buyer has mentioned the alternative directly and whether the account has a contract or a procurement constraint. Update the record with the answer.","Do not use the risk record to pressure the owner or the buyer. The record is a way to prepare a better question, not a reason to criticize the current vendor. A confrontational approach can make the buyer defensive and reduce the chance of learning the real situation. Keep the tone factual and curious."]},{"heading":"Reviewing the risk after a loss","paragraphs":["When an opportunity is lost, check whether the displacement risk was documented and whether the response changed the outcome. A risk that was recorded early but never addressed may point to a process gap. A risk that was missed entirely may point to a research gap. Record the lesson without turning the review into a blame exercise.","Compare the loss with the original risk level. A confirmed risk that led to a loss is a validation of the signal, not a failure of the record. An inferred risk that turned out to be wrong is a reason to tighten the evidence standard. The review should improve the next risk assessment, not just explain the last one."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A risk record is a judgment based on incomplete information. The buyer may not disclose the alternative, and public sources may lag. The evidence level can be interpreted differently by different reviewers. The sample may over-represent accounts with active deals. State these limits when the results are shared.","The record also cannot predict the buyer's final decision. A confirmed alternative may still lose to a better fit, and an inferred risk may never materialize. The team should use the record to ask better questions, not to declare an outcome. A careful risk note improves the conversation without pretending to control it."]}],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Small Business Administration, Market research and competitive analysis","url":"https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/market-research-competitive-analysis"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission, Complying with the Telemarketing Sales Rule","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-telemarketing-sales-rule"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau, Business Trends and Outlook Survey","url":"https://www.census.gov/hfp/btos/"}]},{"slug":"what-does-a-defensible-outbound-suppression-policy-require","title":"What does a defensible outbound suppression policy require?","summary":"A suppression policy checklist with a single source of truth, intake capture, cross-tool propagation, audit evidence, and honest limits.","cluster":"Outreach operations","publishedAt":"2026-11-08","finding":"A defensible outbound suppression policy requires a single authoritative list, capture at every intake point, propagation to every sending tool before the next send, and an audit trail that shows when and why a contact was suppressed.","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-08/what-does-a-defensible-outbound-suppression-policy-require.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","paragraphs":["This brief asks what an outbound suppression policy needs so the team can show that a contact who asked not to be contacted is not contacted again. The policy covers email, phone, social, and any other channel the team uses for outbound work. The scope includes opt-outs, do-not-contact requests, complaints, and internal blocks. It excludes inbound service messages that are required to fulfill an existing contract.","The question matters because a suppression failure is both a trust problem and a compliance risk. A buyer who asks to be removed and then receives another message may complain or report the team. A policy that exists only in a document but not in the sending tools is not a real policy. The evidence has to reach the point where the send happens."]},{"heading":"Define what suppression covers","paragraphs":["List the events that should create a suppression, such as an opt-out, a direct do-not-contact request, a complaint, a legal hold, or an internal decision to stop. Each event should have a definition and an owner. A vague category such as not interested can create confusion, because a buyer may mean not now rather than never. The definition should tell the recorder which scope to apply.","Separate a channel-level suppression from an account-level one. A buyer may ask not to receive email but still accept a phone call, or may ask the team to stop all contact. The record should show the scope of the request and who confirmed it. The broader the suppression, the more care the team should take before overriding it."]},{"heading":"Use one source of truth","paragraphs":["Choose one system as the authoritative suppression list and make every other tool read from it. The source of truth should be accessible to the people who send and to the people who review. A spreadsheet can work for a small team if it is controlled and backed up. A CRM field can work if the sending tools check it before every send.","Do not keep parallel lists that are updated by hand. Parallel lists drift, and the drift is invisible until a failure. If a tool cannot read the source of truth directly, define a manual check and a named owner. The workaround should be temporary and reviewed. A permanent manual step is a permanent risk."]},{"heading":"Capture at every intake point","paragraphs":["Suppression can be requested in a reply, a phone call, a web form, a social message, or a conversation at an event. Each intake point needs a path to the source of truth. The person who receives the request should record it the same day, with the exact wording and the channel. A request that stays in an inbox is not a suppression.","Give the team a short script for confirming the scope of the request. Ask whether the buyer wants to stop all contact or only one channel, and whether the request applies to the whole company or one person. Record the answer without arguing. If the buyer is unsure, choose the broader suppression until they clarify."]},{"heading":"Propagate before the next send","paragraphs":["The suppression should reach every sending tool before the next outbound action. If the tools update overnight, the team needs a rule that blocks sends until the update completes. A send that was already queued when the request arrived should be checked before it goes out. The policy should name who is responsible for the check.","Test the propagation with a controlled contact rather than a real buyer. Create a test record, suppress it, and confirm that every tool blocks the send. Repeat the test when a tool changes or a new channel is added. A policy that has never been tested is a hope, not a control."]},{"heading":"Keep an audit trail","paragraphs":["Record the request date, the channel, the exact wording, the scope, the person who recorded it, and the date the suppression reached each tool. Keep the evidence in a place that a reviewer can access. The trail should show that the team acted, not just that a field is checked. Link the record to the original message so the wording can be verified.","If a suppression is reversed, record who approved the reversal and why. A reversal should be rare and should require a new request from the buyer or a documented legal basis. The history should preserve the original request even after the reversal. A record that erases the past cannot support a review."]},{"heading":"Review and audit","paragraphs":["Review a sample of suppression records each quarter and check that each one reached the source of truth and the sending tools. Look for requests that were recorded but not propagated, and for sends that occurred after a request. A small sample can reveal a broken path before a buyer complains. Record the sample size and the failures so the next review can compare.","Use the audit to test the intake points, not only the records. Send a test request through each channel and follow it to the suppression. If a channel has no path, fix it or stop using it for outbound work. The audit should be documented and repeated on a schedule."]},{"heading":"Handle exceptions carefully","paragraphs":["An exception may be needed for a legal notice, a transactional message, or a request the buyer makes after the suppression. Define the exception in advance and require approval from a named owner. Record the reason and the scope. Do not let an exception become a general permission to contact the buyer again.","If a buyer asks for information after opting out, clarify whether they want a one-time response or a resumption of contact. A one-time response should be limited to the request. A resumption should be recorded as a new permission with a date. The distinction protects the buyer's choice and the team's record."]},{"heading":"Reporting and use","paragraphs":["Report the number of suppression requests by channel, the share that reached the source of truth within the target time, and the number of failed propagation tests. Add a short note on any reversal or exception. These counts show whether the policy is working, not just whether it exists. Keep the report with the audit evidence so a reviewer can trace each number.","Use the findings to fix the weakest intake point or the slowest propagation path. Then test again and compare. The goal is a policy that a reviewer can verify and a buyer can trust. A simple control that is tested is more valuable than a complex policy that is never checked."]},{"heading":"Train the team on suppression","paragraphs":["Train every person who can send or receive an outbound message on the suppression policy. The training should cover how to recognize a request, how to confirm its scope, and where to record it. Use a short scenario and a knowledge check rather than a long document. A person who does not know the path cannot follow it.","Refresh the training when a tool, a channel, or a legal requirement changes. Keep the policy and the training in the same place so the team can find both. Record who completed the training and when. A policy that depends on memory alone will fail when the team is busy or when a new person joins."]},{"heading":"Working with partners and vendors","paragraphs":["If a partner or vendor sends on the team's behalf, the suppression policy must cover their sends too. Share the source of truth or a controlled extract and require confirmation that it was applied. Put the requirement in the contract or the statement of work. A partner who cannot honor the list should not send for the team.","Review the partner's process on a schedule and test a sample of suppressions. A partner may have a different tool or a different definition of opt-out. The team remains responsible for the buyer's experience even when another company sends the message. A clear agreement and a periodic check reduce that risk."]},{"heading":"Limits","paragraphs":["A suppression policy cannot guarantee that every message is stopped if a tool ignores the source of truth or a person sends outside the system. The policy also depends on the buyer's request being recorded accurately. The sample may miss requests that were never captured. State these limits when the results are shared.","The policy should not be used to avoid legitimate communication that the buyer has asked for. A transactional message or a requested follow-up may be appropriate even after a marketing opt-out. The distinction should be defined with legal and operational input. The goal is to honor the buyer's choice, not to create a blanket silence that harms the relationship."]}],"sources":[{"label":"Federal Trade Commission, CAN-SPAM Act compliance guide","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business"},{"label":"Federal Trade Commission, Complying with the Telemarketing Sales Rule","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-telemarketing-sales-rule"},{"label":"National Institute of Standards and Technology, Privacy Framework","url":"https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework"},{"label":"Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Cyber Essentials","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/cyber-essentials"},{"label":"Internal Revenue Service, How long should I keep records","url":"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records"}]}];
